cpm -- Command

Move files between COHERENT and a CP/M-8000 drive
cpm [-f image] function [argument ...]

cpm reads and writes the files on a CP/M-8000 drive from COHERENT, much as
dos(1) does for an MS-DOS file system.  The drive is not mounted and the
kernel is never told its format: cpm walks the CP/M 2.2 directory itself.  By
default it works on /dev/cpma, the raw partition holding drive A: of the
CP/M-8000 system that shares the disk; -f names an image to work on instead,
either another special file or an ordinary file holding a copy of such a
partition.

Drive A:'s layout is compiled in: records of 128 bytes, allocation blocks of
4096 bytes, 2560 blocks, and 512 directory entries filling the first four
blocks.  It is the layout the CP/M-8000 BIOS builds for that drive, so a file
cpm writes is a file CP/M reads.

A CP/M name has eight characters and an extension of three.  cpm folds the
name given to it to upper case and splits it at the first `.'.  It refuses a
name that will not fit those fields, one that is empty, and one holding a
character CP/M forbids: a space, a character outside printable ASCII, or one
of <>.,;:=?*[]" -- every file cpm reads, writes or removes belongs to CP/M
user 0.

Functions
Exactly one of these is given, and it must come after any -f.
    ls		List the drive.  For each file: its user number, name, size in
		bytes, blocks, directory entries, and, where the drive keeps
		them, its creation and update stamps.  Ahead of the files come
		the directory label, any password entry, any entry of a kind
		cpm does not recognise, and the number of date-stamp entries;
		after them, the file count and the total bytes.

    read	Copy cpmfile off the drive into file, or to the standard
		output when no file is named.  CP/M measures a file in whole
		128-byte records, so what comes back is padded out to a record
		boundary and cpm does not strip the padding: nothing on the
		drive records where the file ended.  A 32-kilobyte span a
		sparse file never wrote reads back as NUL bytes.

    write	Copy file onto the drive as cpmfile, replacing any file of
		that name.  A file whose extension is TXT, C, H, SUB, PD, 8KN,
		S, ASM, DOC or MAN is treated as text and its last partial
		record is padded with ^Z, the CP/M end of file; any other file
		is padded with NUL bytes.  The directory is written back only
		once the data and the new entries are complete, so a copy that
		fails part way leaves the drive as it was.

    rm		Remove cpmfile from the drive, with the date stamps kept for
		it.

Examples
The first copies a COHERENT file onto drive A: under a name CP/M can hold,
lists the drive to see that it arrived, and copies it back:
    cpm write /etc/motd MOTD.TXT
    cpm ls
    cpm read MOTD.TXT /tmp/motd

The second reads a file out of an ordinary file holding a copy of the
partition, and writes it to the standard output:
    cpm -f /tmp/cpma.img read HELLO.C

Directory extensions
A drive may carry entries CP/M 2.2 itself knows nothing of: a directory label,
passwords, and date stamps.  The CP/M-8000 BDOS allocates nothing from them
and claims no slot they occupy, so a drive carrying them still runs unmodified
CP/M.  cpm keeps every such entry it finds, including kinds it does not
understand, and when the directory label asks for stamping it stamps the file
it has just written.

Diagnostics
A diagnostic goes to the standard error and the exit status is 1 when the
drive or the file cannot be opened, when the name is not one CP/M can hold,
when a file named for reading or removal is not on the drive, and when the
drive has no room left for the data or for the directory entries a copy needs.
Otherwise the status is 0.

Files
/dev/cpma	drive A: of the CP/M-8000 system on the same disk

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it: cpm was written for this
port, and this page was written here from its source; none of Mark Williams
Company's text is in it.

The size CP/M keeps is a count of records, so a file written to the drive and
read back is longer than the original unless its length is a multiple of 128
bytes.

The layout is compiled in, so -f is worth using only on an image of a drive
laid out like /dev/cpma.

There is no way to name a CP/M user number other than 0.

